Which of the following function types best models the relationship between $x$ and $y$?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"Linear, because the population increases by approximately the same amount every $5$ years"},{"key":"B","text":"Linear, because each population value is greater than the previous value"},{"key":"C","text":"Exponential, because the population increases by approximately the same factor every $5$ years"},{"key":"D","text":"Quadratic, because the differences between consecutive population values are increasing"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'C', NULL, E'Checking the ratios of consecutive $y$-values: $\\frac{310}{200} = 1.55$, $\\frac{482}{310} \\approx 1.555$, and $\\frac{749}{482} \\approx 1.554$. Since the ratio is approximately constant ($\\approx 1.55$), the data follows an exponential model. The option stating the population increases by the same amount every $5$ years is wrong because the differences are $110$, $172$, and $267$, which are not constant. The option claiming the data is linear because values are increasing confuses a necessary condition (increasing values) with a sufficient one — many function types increase. The option suggesting a quadratic model because differences are increasing is incorrect; while the first differences do increase, the hallmark of exponential growth is a constant ratio, and the second differences ($62$ and $95$) are not constant as a quadratic would require.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"B":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"misread_question"},"C":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 90, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);









