For what value of $k$ does the system of equations above have no solution?', NULL, $lyceon_json$[{"key":"A","text":"$-36$"},{"key":"B","text":"$-4$"},{"key":"C","text":"$\\frac{9}{4}$"},{"key":"D","text":"$4$"}]$lyceon_json$::jsonb, 'B', NULL, E'A system of two linear equations has no solution when the lines are parallel but not identical. The coefficients must be proportional — $\\frac{3}{9} = \\frac{k}{-12}$ — while the constants are not: $\\frac{14}{7} \\neq \\frac{3}{9}$. Solving $\\frac{1}{3} = \\frac{k}{-12}$ gives $k = -4$. With $k = -4$ the constant ratio $\\frac{14}{7} = 2$ differs from $\\frac{1}{3}$, confirming no solution. The value $-36$ results from computing the cross product $3 \\times (-12) = -36$ but neglecting to divide by $9$, stopping one step short. The value $\\frac{9}{4}$ comes from setting the slopes to multiply to $-1$ (the perpendicular condition) instead of setting them equal (the parallel condition). The value $4$ has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign, a sign error in the proportion.', $lyceon_json${"A":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"partial_reasoning"},"B":{"role":"correct","error_taxonomy":null},"C":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"concept_gap"},"D":{"role":"distractor","error_taxonomy":"sign_error"}}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, NULL, 'draft', 1, 105, false, $lyceon_json${"provenance":"Lyceon original","authored_by":"claude","authored_date":"2026-08-19"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb, $lyceon_json${"model":"claude","generation_date":"2026-08-19","prompt_version":"questions_governance_v1"}$lyceon_json$::jsonb);


